.docname {Deployment & Kubernetes}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Pod | One running copy of a service | `telemetry-5d8...` |
    | Deployment | Keeps stateless pods running | `k8s/deployments/chat.yml` |
    | StatefulSet | Same, for pods with their own disk | `twin-db-0` |
    | PVC | A disk claimed by a pod | `twin-data-twin-db-0` |
    | Init container | Runs first, must finish before the service starts | `wait-for-db` |
    | k3d | Kubernetes in Docker, for local use | `just k8s create` |

How the cluster is built. Commands and setup: [Kubernetes & Istio](../toolchain/kubernetes.qd).

---

## What is in `k8s/`

```bash
k8s/
├── namespace.yml                     # ambitwin, enrolled in ambient mode
├── istio-gateway.yml                 # the gateway and one route per service
├── istio-peer-authentication.yml     # STRICT mTLS
├── istio-request-authentication.yml  # JWT check at the edge
├── istio-agent-authz.yml             # who may call agent
├── configmaps/common.yml             # shared settings that are not secret
├── deployments/                      # 12 stateless services
├── stateful/                         # 8 databases, Redis, Kafka
└── monitoring/                       # Prometheus, Grafana
```

---

## Stateless or stateful

| Kind | What | If a pod dies |
|---|---|---|
| Deployment | The 12 services | A new one starts anywhere |
| StatefulSet | 4 MongoDB, 4 PostgreSQL, Redis, Kafka | Same name, same disk |

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        S["Headless Service: twin-db"] --> P["Pod: twin-db-0"]
        P --> V["PVC: twin-data-twin-db-0"]

---

## Starting order

A service must not start before its database. Each pod waits in an init container:

```yaml
initContainers:
  - name: wait-for-db
    image: busybox:1.38.0
    command: [sh, -c, "until nc -z twin-db 27017; do sleep 2; done"]
```

- `provisioner` also waits for `registry` and `tenancy`.
- `claims-gateway` waits for `tenancy`.
- The check only proves the port is open, not that the service is healthy.

---

## Settings and secrets

- **Shared settings:** `k8s/configmaps/common.yml`.
- **Secrets:** `just k8s secrets` reads `.env` and pipes them into `kubectl apply`. No file is written.
- **One exception:** `claims-gateway-key`, a mounted PEM file.

.box type:{warning}
    A Kubernetes secret is base64, not encryption. RBAC is the real guard. For production, prefer an external manager.

---

## Hardening on every service

- `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`
- Read-only root filesystem, with `emptyDir` at `/tmp` where needed
- Requests and limits set
- Liveness and readiness probes

---

## Updates

```bash
kubectl rollout restart deployment/digital-twin -n ambitwin
kubectl rollout status  deployment/digital-twin -n ambitwin
kubectl rollout undo    deployment/digital-twin -n ambitwin
```

The old pod serves until the new one is ready.

Application images use `IfNotPresent`, so `just k8s load` works on k3d without a registry. For a real cluster see [Trust & Service Mesh](service-mesh.qd).

---

## Known gaps

- **One replica everywhere.** No autoscaler, no `PodDisruptionBudget`. Losing a pod is an outage.
- **Databases are single instances** with no backup defined here.
- **`:latest` tags** mean a rollout may not pick up new code.

---

## When something is wrong

```bash
# why is the pod not ready? read Events at the bottom
kubectl describe pod <pod> -n ambitwin

# logs: current, previous crash, init container
kubectl logs deployment/digital-twin -n ambitwin
kubectl logs <pod> -n ambitwin --previous
kubectl logs <pod> -n ambitwin -c wait-for-db

# shell inside
kubectl exec -it <pod> -n ambitwin -- sh

# recent events
kubectl get events -n ambitwin --sort-by=.lastTimestamp

# which image is really running
kubectl get pod <pod> -n ambitwin -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].image}'
```

k3d refuses connections after a restart? Find the port mapped to 6443:

```bash
docker ps --filter name=k3d-ambitwin-serverlb
kubectl config set-cluster k3d-ambitwin --server=https://127.0.0.1:<PORT>
```
